WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:03.630
(missile hissing)

00:00:03.630 --> 00:00:04.740
- [Narrator] A missile
that can be launched

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.780
from a train or a hypersonic one

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:08.490
that can evade defenses.

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:10.070
These are some of the newest weapons

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:11.580
in North Korea's arsenal,

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.700
which is already stocked
with nuclear bombs

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:15.610
and intercontinental ballistic missiles

00:00:15.610 --> 00:00:18.080
that could reach anywhere
on the US mainland.

00:00:18.080 --> 00:00:20.130
- So what they're investing in

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.620
is to make these missiles as creative

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:25.420
and surprising as possible.

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.970
- [Narrator] It's all part
of North Korean leader,

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:29.913
Kim Jong-un's promise to
build an invincible military.

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:33.580
And the growing number of
weapons is drawing attention

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:35.950
from the US and its allies in the region.

00:00:35.950 --> 00:00:37.390
- It's all part of a strategy

00:00:37.390 --> 00:00:38.830
for North Korea to show

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:41.310
that despite years of sanctions

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:43.790
and the struggles of COVID,

00:00:43.790 --> 00:00:46.883
that, at least in terms of
military, it remains strong.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.390
- [Narrator] So here's a rundown of some

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:51.700
of the latest technology
in North Korea's arsenal

00:00:51.700 --> 00:00:54.343
and what message its
expansion sends to the world.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.010
North Korean weapons
tests have taken place

00:00:58.010 --> 00:01:01.220
under all three generations
of the Kim family leadership.

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:04.530
The country launched its first
home-grown missile in 1984

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.630
but the frequency of the tests
spiked under Kim Jong-un,

00:01:07.630 --> 00:01:09.630
and included one of the
largest-ever launched

00:01:09.630 --> 00:01:10.998
by the country.

00:01:10.998 --> 00:01:13.998
(missile whooshing)

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:16.400
Tests slowed during the pandemic

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:17.960
but in September of this year,

00:01:17.960 --> 00:01:19.680
North Korea unveiled a new weapon

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.540
that would be the first in
a quick succession of tests.

00:01:22.540 --> 00:01:24.140
- Most recently, we've seen things

00:01:24.140 --> 00:01:26.360
like long-rang cruise missiles.

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:28.090
- [Narrator] Melissa
Hanham researches weapons

00:01:28.090 --> 00:01:30.860
of mass destruction at
Stanford University's Center

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.410
for International
Security and Cooperation.

00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:35.170
She's been closely watching the activity

00:01:35.170 --> 00:01:37.520
in East Asia for 15 years.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:39.280
- They're almost like a small airplane

00:01:39.280 --> 00:01:41.080
that can turn corners,

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.930
fly through valleys and most importantly,

00:01:43.930 --> 00:01:47.600
fly beneath and around radar.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:49.020
- [Narrator] Hanham says this quality

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:50.860
makes a long-range
cruise missile different

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:52.540
from a ballistic missile,

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:54.710
which can often carry a heavier payload

00:01:54.710 --> 00:01:56.190
and is typically shot into space

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:58.220
with a rocket, then falls in a faster

00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:00.490
but more predictable trajectory.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:01.990
The cruise missile, on the other hand,

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:05.360
can evade detection and
stealthily hit a target.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:08.100
- South Korea itself has been building up

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.480
very powerful Hyunmoo cruise missiles,

00:02:11.480 --> 00:02:14.210
some of which they have
touted historically

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:15.720
could hit Kim Jong-un.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:19.430
So in a way, North Korea's probably trying

00:02:19.430 --> 00:02:23.523
to keep pace with the missiles
coming out of South Korea.

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:26.320
- [Narrator] About two
weeks after the launch

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:27.730
of the long-range cruise missile,

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:30.536
North Korea said it
tested another new weapon.

00:02:30.536 --> 00:02:34.453
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:39.970
- [Melissa] It goes much, much faster

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:40.940
than a cruise missile.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.650
That's why they emphasize hypersonic.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:44.960
- [Narrator] Hypersonic missiles generally

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:46.770
can fly a mile per second,

00:02:46.770 --> 00:02:49.253
traveling about five
times the speed of sound.

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:54.080
- It can maneuver and then dart down

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:55.340
through the atmosphere again

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:57.380
in such a way that it would be very hard

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.880
for ballistic missile defenses

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.340
to track and then intercept.

00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:03.680
And so it's a way of overwhelming

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:07.610
and beating US ballistic missile defenses.

00:03:07.610 --> 00:03:08.443
- [Narrator] Here you can see

00:03:08.443 --> 00:03:10.220
the hypersonic missile on display

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:13.760
while Kim Jong-un surveyed his
expanding arsenal in October.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:15.220
South Korea's military said

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:17.380
that the apparent
hypersonic missile appears

00:03:17.380 --> 00:03:19.650
to be in an early stage of development

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:21.750
but Hanham says the regime
will continue working

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:22.990
on more nimble missiles

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:25.090
that can carry nuclear warheads.

00:03:25.090 --> 00:03:26.440
- [Melissa] North Korea is thought

00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:29.340
to only have a few warheads.

00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:32.760
And that means that they
need to find creative ways

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:36.260
to deliver these warheads overseas.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:38.599
And Kim Jong-un has declared

00:03:38.599 --> 00:03:41.820
that they will have a multitude of ways

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:42.993
of delivering these.

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:45.343
- [Narrator] That also means

00:03:45.343 --> 00:03:47.070
having missile deployment systems,

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:49.280
and the regime's scientists
have also been working

00:03:49.280 --> 00:03:50.770
on new projects.

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:52.770
In September, state media released footage

00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.170
of two short-range ballistic
missiles being launched

00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:56.075
from a train.

00:03:56.075 --> 00:03:58.560
(missile hissing)

00:03:58.560 --> 00:03:59.960
- The idea behind it

00:03:59.960 --> 00:04:03.490
isn't really that it's
a more powerful missile

00:04:03.490 --> 00:04:05.270
or faster missile,

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:07.890
the idea is that it's a sneaky missile.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:08.723
- [Narrator] And that's thanks

00:04:08.723 --> 00:04:10.840
to the country's
extensive railway network,

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:12.850
which stretches from
the demilitarized zone

00:04:12.850 --> 00:04:14.390
that separates the two Koreas

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:16.370
through the country's mountainous terrain

00:04:16.370 --> 00:04:19.070
and all the way to its
border with China and Russia.

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:21.530
- [Melissa] The reason
they've chosen rail cars,

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:23.630
it become nearly impossible

00:04:23.630 --> 00:04:26.237
for the US and its allies to track

00:04:26.237 --> 00:04:28.310
which rail cars have missiles

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:29.683
and which ones don't.

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:34.440
- [Narrator] Another way to
surprise rivals, go under water.

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.720
While submarine launches are not new,

00:04:36.720 --> 00:04:39.010
Pyongyang said its latest tests in October

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:41.230
showcased a missile with advanced features

00:04:41.230 --> 00:04:43.690
like gliding skip mobility.

00:04:43.690 --> 00:04:47.800
- A submarine gives them
one more creative way

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:51.920
of moving around and using
up intelligence resources

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:54.850
as the United States and its allies guess

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:56.800
where these missiles are.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:57.633
- [Narrator] But it isn't known

00:04:57.633 --> 00:05:00.350
if North Korea fired the
missile from an actual submarine

00:05:00.350 --> 00:05:01.770
or from a submerged barge

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:05.150
as Pyongyang is believed to
have done in earlier tests.

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:06.550
- There's a big difference

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:08.530
between testing this type of technology,

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:11.269
and then have it be reliably operational

00:05:11.269 --> 00:05:14.109
or useful in a military context.

00:05:14.109 --> 00:05:15.850
(inspirational national music)

00:05:15.850 --> 00:05:17.010
- [Narrator] But North
Koreans analysts say

00:05:17.010 --> 00:05:18.780
the recent military muscle flexing

00:05:18.780 --> 00:05:21.030
still sends a strong message.

00:05:21.030 --> 00:05:22.410
- North Korea and Kim Jong-un,

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.170
they're still walking this fine line.

00:05:24.170 --> 00:05:25.890
They're not doing something so extreme

00:05:25.890 --> 00:05:28.020
like a nuclear test or an ICBM launch

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:29.938
that really would draw the wrath

00:05:29.938 --> 00:05:31.710
of the entire world

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:34.570
but it has the effect

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:37.150
of just reminding the US and
South Korea in particular

00:05:37.150 --> 00:05:41.230
that we're not gonna stop
until we get recognition

00:05:41.230 --> 00:05:42.763
in the International world.

00:05:44.680 --> 00:05:46.520
- [Narrator] The Biden
administration has said it's willing

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:48.960
to hold talks any time with Pyongyang.

00:05:48.960 --> 00:05:50.950
But in a speech at the weapons exhibit,

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:53.680
Kim said the US couldn't be trusted.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.120
- So if and when talks do
reemerge with South Korea

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.270
or the US, they can promise
to stop doing these types

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:03.020
of tests in return for
some type of goodie,

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:06.260
be it sanctions relief or
humanitarian assistance

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.640
or a peace declaration.

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:10.090
But these weapons tests,

00:06:10.090 --> 00:06:13.430
beyond sharpening North Korea's
ability to defend itself,

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:16.437
and perhaps intimidate
US allies in the region,

00:06:16.437 --> 00:06:20.203
it also gives them a chip
at the bargaining table.

